<article class="env-lemma" id="0FHP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FHP">Lemma <span data-tag="0FHP">45.10.1</span>.</a> Assume given (D0), (D1), (D2), and (D3) satisfying (A), (B), and (C). Let $X, Y$ be nonempty smooth projective schemes both equidimensional of dimension $d$ over $k$. Then $\int _{X \amalg Y} = \int _ X + \int _ Y$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Denote $i : X \to X \amalg Y$ and $j : Y \to X \amalg Y$ be the coprojections. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0FHJ" data-tag="0FHJ">45.9.9</a> the map $(i^*, j^*) : H^*(X \amalg Y) \to H^*(X) \times H^*(Y)$ is an isomorphism. The statement of the lemma means that under the isomorphism $(i^*, j^*) : H^{2d}(X \amalg Y)(d) \to H^{2d}(X)(d) \oplus H^{2d}(Y)(d)$ the map $\int _ X + \int _ Y$ is transformed into $\int _{X \amalg Y}$. This is true because 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \int _{X \amalg Y} a = \int _{X \amalg Y} i_*(i^*a) + j_*(j^*a) = \int _ X i^*a + \int _ Y j^*a  \]
</div>
    <p>
       where the equality $a = i_*(i^*a) + j_*(j^*a)$ was shown in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0FHJ" data-tag="0FHJ">45.9.9</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>